-- title:
--   Ideal cutoffs at a level
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix a tree evaluation, an input $x$ and a common point $b$.
--
--   1. **Ideal cutoff.** For a node $v$ at level $k$, the level-$k$ assignment
--   $$
--   u\mapsto\begin{cases}f_{k,u}(x)&u<v,\\ b&u\ge v.\end{cases}
--   $$
--   It is the idealized state immediately after the whole descendant block of $v$ has switched. The candidate construction identifies its evaluation with an earlier prediction. The before- and after-block root states of the switching experiment are then compared with this ideal state, and the terminal detector is applied.
